            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Scenario: $500 Spent, Zero Conversions</h2>
<p>A utility app team running Meta ads in the US market tested two creative sets:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creative A</strong>: Feature showcase + promotional copy + 60% discount badge</li>
<li><strong>Creative B</strong>: Real user scenario video + pain point hook + natural feature introduction</li>
</ul>
<p>After two weeks, Creative A consumed over $400, delivered 12,000 impressions daily, and achieved a 3.8% CTR—but zero conversions. Creative B spent less than $100 and generated 3 paid actions.</p>
<p>The team started doubting whether Meta ads worked for their product. The real issue wasn't the platform—it was confusing ads that 'can run' with ads that 'can sell'.</p>
<h2>The Core Difference: Running vs. Selling</h2>
<p>'Can run' means the ad gets approved, receives traffic, and gets seen. 'Can sell' means users actually complete your desired action—download, sign up, purchase.</p>
<p>Between these two lies a conversion gap:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Impressions → Clicks → Retention → Conversion</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A 'runnable' ad can capture attention through aesthetics, gimmicks, or discounts—but won't necessarily persuade users to take the next step.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Analyze Funnel Data to Find the Bottleneck</h2>
<p>Back to our case. After exporting ad set-level data from Meta Ads Manager:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Creative A</th>
<th>Creative B</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Spend</td>
<td>$423</td>
<td>$98</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Impressions</td>
<td>11,200</td>
<td>2,300</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>3.8%</td>
<td>5.2%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Click-to-Conversion</td>
<td>12%</td>
<td>28%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversions</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong>: Creative A's CTR was acceptable. The bottleneck was post-click behavior—users clicked but didn't complete sign-up or purchase.</p>
<p>This typically means the landing page experience doesn't match the ad's promise, or the creative set up incorrect expectations. The 60% discount drove clicks, but the landing page's product presentation had zero persuasion power. Users bounced immediately.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Look Beyond CTR to Spot False Positives</h2>
<p>Many marketers judge creatives solely by click-through rate—and miss critical issues. <strong>High CTR + Low Conversion = Creative is 'Click-Baiting'</strong>.</p>
<p>Common causes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Overly aggressive discounts</strong>: Words like DISCOUNT, PROMO, or FREE boost CTR but attract deal-seekers, not target users.</li>
<li><strong>Exaggerated comparisons</strong>: Before/After or price comparison videos spark curiosity clicks, but users find the product underwhelming and leave.</li>
<li><strong>Creative style mismatches the audience</strong>: Users in certain regions are accustomed to fast-paced content. Polished but slow-burn brand videos get abandoned.</li>
</ol>
<p>For utility apps, Creative B's approach is more effective: hook target users with pain point scenarios, then build trust with authentic usage footage. Users clicking already understand what problem the product solves, making conversion intent naturally higher.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Tier Your Response for Smarter Decision-Making</h2>
<p>Don't rush to pause everything when you spot 'runs but doesn't sell' creatives. Tier your response based on performance:</p>
<p><strong>Pause Immediately</strong><br />
- Spent over $100, CTR above 2%, but click conversion below 5%<br />
- These creatives are likely wasting budget</p>
<p><strong>Monitor Continuously</strong><br />
- Moderate spend with visible drop-off points in the conversion path<br />
- Use heatmaps or Meta Event tracking tools to confirm where users leave</p>
<p><strong>Iterate Quickly</strong><br />
- Found a creative delivering 1-2 conversions? That's your 'seed creative'<br />
- Rapidly replicate variants based on converting creative elements (BGM, voiceover pacing, CTA placement)</p>
<h2>Case Result: ROAS Improved 3x in 2 Weeks</h2>
<p>Back to our team. After strategic adjustments, they took three actions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Paused Creative A</strong> and tested a new combination: lower discount + stronger feature showcase</li>
<li><strong>Scaled Creative B's</strong> budget while generating 3 similar variants (different lengths, opening frames)</li>
<li><strong>Optimized the landing page</strong> to ensure promised features appear above the fold</li>
</ol>
<p>Two weeks later, the ad set's ROAS climbed from 0.2 to 0.85—not profitable yet, but cost per conversion dropped 40% and overall campaign health improved significantly.</p>
<h2>The Single Metric That Matters Most</h2>
<p>If time for review is limited, focus on one metric: <strong>Post-click Conversion Rate</strong>.</p>
<p>This directly reflects alignment between creative and landing experience. Above 15% suggests the creative's promise matches delivery. Below 5% signals a problem with user expectation management.</p>
<p><strong>A creative that runs isn't necessarily a good creative. One that sells is.</strong> Distinguishing between these two is the critical step in turning paid campaigns from money pits into profit generators.</p>
